A||||3||86SE||CODE 13||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||02-05-2000||11:12 PM||||My V-6 engine has 2 stored codes, 13 and 32.<P>The haynes manual says the ECM stores a code 13 if the O2 sensor is open.  What do they mean by "open"??, and open circuit??<P>As for the code 32, i can warm up my engine and push up on the EGR valve and the engine doesnt stall.  I can push the thing inside the EGR valve up with rather moderate force.  I get about 18-20 MPG on the Highway, if I replace both the O2 sensor and the EGR valve will my codes go away and will i get better gas milage???||||reg||5
Z||000001||daved||02-05-2000||11:36 PM||||86SE,    Open circuit usually just means that the wire is broken or disconnected. In this case, ECM thinks the O2 sensor is "open" because it probably is not sending any output voltage. You can actually measure the output with the engine running. Hook the red meter wire to the bare connector end of the sensor, and the black one to ground. After a short time, the voltage should read (approx.) between 400 to 800mV (thats milliVolts, or 0.800 of a Volt) Anything substantially out of this range, and its time for a new one. Note that the sensor output is dependant on the mixture, so it will vary.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierosound||02-06-2000||06:09 PM||||I have a code 13 as well, so I replaced the O2 sensor and cleared the ECM.  I then drove the car for a day or so and rechecked the ECM for codes.  I still have a code 13!!  According to the manuals, there can be 3 things wrong; the sensor, the wiring from the sensor to ECM, and the ECM itself.  I'll have to look at it more, when the weather warms up.||||reg||10
Z||000003||terryk||02-06-2000||09:13 PM||||Code 13 is caused when the O2 voltage is in the cold voltage range of .35V to .45V when there is no dang reason for it to be.<P>Per the manual:<P>Stays within .35-.45V<BR>TPS above .5V<BR>Engine runtime over 1 minute<BR>Coolant over 85C. (about 180F?)<BR>RPM over 1600.<P>Basically, the ECM thinks you are driving and expects the O2 to start cycling after a minute. It doesn't and throws that code.<P>Either the O2 is disconnected or dead. I'd go with dead.<P>One thing you can do is disconnect the O2, start the engine and ground the O2 lead going back to the ECM. This will tell the ECM the mixture is lean and the engine will change how it is running. It will get very rich and may flood. <P>If grounding the lead changes how it runs, the connection back to the ECM is good. You can try the other way with a 1.5V battery. This will cause a rich condition and the ECM will lean the fuel out.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-08-2000).]||||reg||1
